#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Behavior specification
# ======================
# This standalone Python 3, standard-library CLI prepares and verifies this
# Neovim configuration. It owns dependency acquisition and builds; Neovim
# startup consumes the prepared runtime instead of installing or repairing it.
# Repository paths are anchored to this script, not the working directory.
# Explicit scan paths are the exception: relative paths use the caller's cwd.
# Tools use the inherited environment and PATH, with existing local bin
# directories prepended. The config link is $HOME/.config/nvim, not an
# XDG_CONFIG_HOME-derived path; an existing conflicting entry is not replaced.
#
# Inputs and managed state
# ------------------------
# - source-manifest.json declares Git URLs, exact commits, repository/worktree
#   locations, sparse selections (which source paths to check out), permitted
#   generated paths, native builds, and Tree-sitter parser/query inputs.
#   The loader validates these declarations before source consumers use them.
# - Python and Node resolution stays in uv/pnpm. Bound component inventories
#   verify installed bytes without those managers. Missing required state fails;
#   healthy Python environments remain valid without local wheel caches.
#   uv-derived hashed registry requirements and standard wheels support local
#   pip-free reconstruction. VCS/direct-URL/index-changing inputs are rejected.
# - Managed Git sources use bare repositories plus detached working copies,
#   with worktree locks and sparse selections as declared. Pinning a commit is
#   not permission to discard local source edits. allowedDirtyPaths identifies
#   the exceptions accepted by cleanliness checks and permitted native outputs.
# - vendor/plugins contains checked-in, trimmed plugin snapshots. Installation
#   does not acquire, update, copy, or repair them; check verifies their layout
#   and provenance metadata. Plugin specifications remain in init.lua.
# - Derived outputs are native plugin artifacts in permitted worktree paths,
#   compiled parsers and assembled queries in the declared Tree-sitter runtime,
#   and an offline schema catalog built from the local schemastore source.
#   Hash-pinned release archives can supply Tree-sitter inputs instead of Git
#   files; both the archive and its extracted cache are verified.
#
# Command behavior
# ----------------
# install:
#   Reuse verified production packages or prepare them through uv/pnpm.
#   Preserve a usable dev-synced Python environment rather than prune its tools.
#   Then ensure pinned sources, release inputs, catalog, native artifacts, and
#   Tree-sitter outputs, and ensure the Neovim config link. Current managed state
#   skips source/build/runtime work, but not package checks or config linking.
# install --refresh:
#   Bypass that whole-state fast path and refresh Git refs before ensuring the
#   same manifest pins. This does not choose newer commits or force rebuilds
#   of artifacts whose own state checks still pass.
# install --offline:
#   Reuse valid packages or reconstruct Python from verified local wheels;
#   never invoke uv/pnpm, acquire remotely, or rebuild native plugins. Disable
#   Git lazy fetching/protocol acquisition. Valid parsers need no build CLI;
#   stale runtime can rebuild from local inputs/tools, but final validation is
#   never skipped. --offline --refresh remains local-only. Link only after all
#   required components pass the same checks as normal installation.
# archive --output PATH:
#   Export verified ordinary state for declared Linux/macOS architecture profiles.
#   Never acquire, repair, change pins, or package a virtualenv/configuration.
#   Require ordinary wheels for supported Python minors; preserve existing
#   managed license placement. Sanitize Git metadata only in archive bytes;
#   normalize packaging and reject unsafe links/types/permissions/destinations.
#   This is not an importer or a promise of reproducible native compilation.
# check [--fix]:
#   Verify managed prerequisites and checked-in plugin snapshots, then run Ruff,
#   ty, Python regressions, StyLua, and Lua language-server checks. With --fix,
#   formatters and Ruff's safe fixes can edit project code. Run the explicit
#   Plenary Neovim test harness only after prerequisite/development checks pass.
#   Do not install missing development tools or synchronize their environments.
# scan [--strict] [paths...]:
#   Run Semgrep using active rules/ configurations on supplied targets or the
#   existing managed vendor directories. .disabled rule files are not loaded.
#   ERROR findings fail by default; --strict also selects WARNING findings and
#   never weakens ERROR enforcement. Scanner failures fail the command, but
#   scanner-reported timeouts/skipped files can still accompany a zero exit.
#
# Safety, freshness, and failure boundaries
# -----------------------------------------
# - Managed paths stay within their owning roots, including resolved symlink
#   targets. Source repositories/worktrees do not overlap derived-runtime roots.
#   Native output and dependency-overlay paths stay within allowedDirtyPaths.
# - Native builds wait for all managed Git sources, including declared build
#   dependencies, to be ready. Tree-sitter builds also require ready release
#   inputs. Native builds recheck output paths and source cleanliness before
#   recording a successful build revision.
# - Freshness is more than existence: source identity, build/configuration
#   metadata, platform/deployment requirements, output hashes, and generation
#   signatures govern reuse. Tree-sitter
#   installation and checking share one resolved output plan and exact file
#   inventory. Generation-rule changes need the relevant generator-version bump.
# - Stale native libraries and stamps are removed before rebuilding and after
#   failed/skipped builds. Optional native absence preserves runtime fallback;
#   it is not a current build, and a leftover stamp without its artifact fails
#   state checking. Required native absence is a failure.
# - Build Tree-sitter/catalog outputs in staging; validate required Tree-sitter
#   files/signatures and catalog serialization before publication. Publish owned
#   directories with backups and rollback attempts; preserve the release cache.
#   Report recovery/cleanup failures and retained backup locations. The entire
#   install is not a transaction: earlier successful work can remain after a
#   later failure. Required-state failure prevents configuration linking.
# - Install/check do not implicitly run scan or perform supply-chain review.
#   Review candidate dependency revisions and scan them before loading their
#   runtime code. Apart from check's explicit Plenary harness, Neovim invocations
#   here use --clean for built-in queries, not dependency execution/builds.
# - Commands return 0 for their defined success state and 1 for reported
#   operational/check failures; argparse handles usage errors separately.
#   A successful install is not proof that all optional tools/features exist.
#   A successful scan is not proof of safety or complete dependency coverage.
#
